{"slug":"quercus-robur","plant":{"taxon":"Quercus robur","family":"Fagaceae","rank":"species","gbif_key":2878688,"wikidata":"Q165145","iucn":"LC","gbif_occurrences":1736601,"common_name":"pedunculate oak","names":[{"source":"culpeper","name":"The Oak","monograph_id":"cul:217-the-oak","confidence":"high","note":"English oak; no description."}],"n_compounds":85,"top_compounds":[{"name":"vanillin","qid":"Q33495"},{"name":"(E)-p-coumaric acid","qid":"Q99374"},{"name":"phenol","qid":"Q130336"},{"name":"oxalic acid","qid":"Q184832"},{"name":"pyrocatechol","qid":"Q282440"},{"name":"abscisic acid","qid":"Q332211"},{"name":"gallic acid","qid":"Q375837"},{"name":"pyrogallol","qid":"Q388692"}],"epmc_total":1629,"trials":1,"claims":[{"concept_id":"hemoptysis","dsc":0,"cul":2,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.46,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"kidney_disease","dsc":0,"cul":2,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":1,"lift":0.09,"evidence_tier":"sparse"},{"concept_id":"diarrhea","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":1,"lift":0.25,"evidence_tier":"sparse"},{"concept_id":"diuresis","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.17,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"dysentery","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.38,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"envenomation","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.48,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"fever","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":1,"lift":0.13,"evidence_tier":"sparse"},{"concept_id":"hemorrhage","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":6,"lift":0.3,"evidence_tier":"studied"},{"concept_id":"inflammation","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":7,"lift":0.18,"evidence_tier":"studied"},{"concept_id":"itch_scabies","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.25,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"leukorrhea","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.5,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"liver_disease","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":2,"lift":0.13,"evidence_tier":"sparse"},{"concept_id":"menorrhagia","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.56,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"nausea_vomiting","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.09,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"plague","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":3,"lift":0.56,"evidence_tier":"sparse"},{"concept_id":"poisoning","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":6,"lift":0.95,"evidence_tier":"studied"},{"concept_id":"sexual_function","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.21,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"urolithiasis","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.29,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"uterine_disorders","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":0,"lift":0.04,"evidence_tier":"unstudied"},{"concept_id":"wounds","dsc":0,"cul":1,"persistent":false,"epmc_pair":7,"lift":0.53,"evidence_tier":"studied"}],"lead_score":null},"texts":{"cul:217-the-oak":{"ref":"Culpeper, Complete Herbal: The Oak","headword":"The Oak","lang":"en","source":"culpeper","text":"Jupiter owns the tree. The leaves and bark of the Oak, and the acorn cups, do bind and dry very much. The inner bark of the tree, and the thin skin that covers the acorn, are most used to stay the spitting of blood, and the bloody-flux. The decoction of that bark, and the powder of the cups, do stay vomitings, spitting of blood, bleeding at the mouth, or other fluxes of blood, in men or women; lasks also, and the nocturnal involuntary flux of men. The acorn in powder taken in wine, provokes urine, and resists the poison of venomous creatures. The decoction of acorns and the bark made in milk and taken, resists the force of poisonous herbs and medicines, as also the virulency of cantharides, when one by eating them hath his bladder exulcerated, and voids bloody urine. Hippocrates saith, he used the fumes of Oak leaves to women that were troubled with the strangling of the mother; and Galen applied them, being bruised, to cure green wounds. The distilled water of the Oaken bud, before they break out into leaves is good to be used either inwardly or outwardly, to assuage inflammations, and to stop all manner of fluxes in man or woman. The same is singularly good in pestilential and hot burning fevers; for it resists the force of the infection, and allays the heat: It cools the heat of the liver, breaking the stone in the kidneys, and stays women’s courses. The decoction of the leaves works the same effects. The water that is found in the hollow places of old Oaks, is very effectual against any foul or spreading scabs. The distilled water (or concoction, which is better) of the leaves, is one of the best remedies that I know of for the whites in women.","spans":[{"start":197,"end":214,"concept_id":"hemoptysis","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":224,"end":235,"concept_id":"dysentery","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":301,"end":310,"concept_id":"nausea_vomiting","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":312,"end":329,"concept_id":"hemoptysis","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":331,"end":339,"concept_id":"hemorrhage","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":363,"end":378,"concept_id":"hemorrhage","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":397,"end":402,"concept_id":"diarrhea","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":427,"end":450,"concept_id":"sexual_function","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":487,"end":501,"concept_id":"diuresis","polarity":"action"},{"start":529,"end":537,"concept_id":"envenomation","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":631,"end":646,"concept_id":"poisoning","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":733,"end":740,"concept_id":"kidney_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":764,"end":776,"concept_id":"kidney_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":880,"end":890,"concept_id":"uterine_disorders","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":945,"end":951,"concept_id":"wounds","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1085,"end":1098,"concept_id":"inflammation","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1181,"end":1193,"concept_id":"plague","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1210,"end":1216,"concept_id":"fever","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1295,"end":1312,"concept_id":"liver_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1327,"end":1332,"concept_id":"urolithiasis","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1340,"end":1347,"concept_id":"kidney_disease","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1353,"end":1374,"concept_id":"menorrhagia","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1534,"end":1539,"concept_id":"itch_scabies","polarity":"therapeutic"},{"start":1660,"end":1666,"concept_id":"leukorrhea","polarity":"therapeutic"}]}},"gloss":{"cul:217-the-oak":{"197":"spitting of blood","224":"bloody flux: dysentery","301":"stays vomiting / vomits","312":"spitting of blood","331":"bleeding (inward / outward / of wounds)","363":"fluxes of blood","397":"lask: diarrhoea, looseness of the bowels","427":"nocturnal pollutions; priapism (continual standing of the yard)","487":"provokes urine: diuretic","529":"venomous beasts / creatures","631":"poisonous herbs / qualities","733":"bladder","764":"bloody urine: haematuria","880":"the mother: the womb (fits, rising, suffocation, pains of the mother)","945":"wounds (green, fresh, inward, old); wound-herb","1085":"inflammations","1181":"pestilence, pestilential fevers / sores","1210":"fevers","1295":"obstructions / heat of the liver","1327":"the stone (urinary calculus)","1340":"kidneys","1353":"stays / stops women's courses, the terms","1534":"scabs: scabies / scabby skin","1660":"the whites: leucorrhoea"}},"images":[]}